n brought in to adjudicated  asst  buuuut we rely on the needs of others to  remind us of what we're capable what  holds true in professional life applies  as much to personal experience the best  way to charm and break the ice with a  new person we like the look of in a  public place isn't to try to say  something witty or soothing we should  strive to bring them a question we  should ask them whether we're in the  right line whether they know when the  post office opens or if they have any  idea how long chicken this size might  need in an oven with closer friends - we  should dare to reveal our amusement we  should ask them if they could possibly  spare a moment then solicit their views  on what we might do about our angry  teenage child or how we should cope with  a sexless relationship or what they'd  advise us about a colleague who's prone  to panic our questions won't probably be  a burden they will show that we're ready  to make ourselves vulnerable in their  eyes and therefore that we trust and  think them wise this isn't just a  cynical strategy for ingratiating  ourselves it isn't Machiavellian or sly  we genuinely all need help we aren't  pretending to have problems and making  up a few just a flutter we're suffering  inside but simply generally don't dare  to reveal the truth for fear of driving  people away and yet we are staying  guarded out of an ideal of  self-sufficiency that isn't either true  to our needs or constructive for the  well-being and esteem of others so we  should risk doing what we at heart have  always longed to do to reveal some of  the fear sadness and angst we genuinely  feel to those we care about we will be  helped in our pain we will remind others  of their capacities and if we're  fortunate  we'll set a precedent that means that  others will one day bring a few of their  problems to us in turn  thank you for watching remember to like  the video and please subscribe to our  channel for more  you
 

